<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>

    <!-- NuGet package metadata is immutable once a version is published, so this has to be
         right before the first upload — set here, not per-project, because InTest.Cli and
         InTest.Runtime are the only two packed projects and both want the same values. This
         applies repo-wide, including the four test projects and four sample APIs; all eight
         already set <IsPackable>false</IsPackable> individually (verified by inspecting every
         .csproj under tests/ and samples/ during the ownership-transfer change that added these
         properties), so the assembly-level Authors/Copyright attributes land in their DLLs like
         any other project's, but none of them can be packed or published. -->
    <Authors>Tjay Ojo</Authors>
    <Copyright>Copyright (c) 2026 Tjay Ojo</Copyright>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Tjayojo/intest</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Tjayojo/intest.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>

    <!-- SourceLink itself is NOT set here: the .NET 10 SDK imports it implicitly for a repo whose
         RepositoryUrl (above) points at GitHub, and referencing Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub
         explicitly would suppress that implicit import and replace a working in-box mechanism
         with an out-of-band copy — see docs/superpowers/specs/
         2026-08-23-nuget-publish-readiness-design.md §2. What's set here is what the implicit
         import does not cover: symbol packaging and deterministic-build gating. -->
    <EmbedUntrackedSources>true</EmbedUntrackedSources>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

    <!-- Runs on InTest.Runtime only in practice: Microsoft.NET.PackTool.targets hard-sets this
         false for PackAsTool projects (InTest.Cli), so it is a no-op there by SDK design, not an
         oversight here — see the readiness spec §6. -->
    <EnablePackageValidation>true</EnablePackageValidation>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- [version-from-git] (docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-trunk-based-versioning.md, Task 2):
       there is no <Version> element here any more. It used to be pinned to "0.1.0" — first as
       the product's own version, later (after Task 1's [scaffold-reads-itself]) purely to serve
       InitCommand.cs's scaffold string, which by then read CliVersion.Current instead of this
       element and had nothing left to compare it against. Both roles are gone: MinVer (referenced
       build-time-only, PrivateAssets="all", by src/InTest.Cli and src/InTest.Runtime only — see
       those .csproj files) now derives Version, PackageVersion, AssemblyVersion and
       InformationalVersion from git tags and commit height at build time, and
       PackageVersionCouplingTests.ReadRuntimeSelfVersion reads CliVersion.Current's own build
       output instead of this file.

       Measured on SDK 10.0.400, MinVer 7.0.0, in a scratch clone of this repository (see this
       task's own report for the exact commands run):
         untagged commits on main   -> 0.1.0-preview.0.<height>  (height = commits since the
                                        nearest ancestor tag, or since the root commit if none)
         exactly on tag "0.1.0"     -> 0.1.0, clean — no prerelease suffix
         a commit after that tag    -> 0.2.0-preview.0.<height>  (MinVerAutoIncrement=minor bumps
                                        the minor component for untagged commits past a release)
         tagged "0.2.0-preview.1"   -> 0.2.0-preview.1, exactly as tagged
       That is exactly [tag-is-the-release]'s three behaviours, with no CI logic and no counter to
       choose or hand-maintain.

       MinVerMinimumMajorMinor floors the untagged-root-commit case at 0.1 rather than MinVer's
       own default of 0.0.0-alpha.0 — otherwise this repository's very first commit, which
       predates any tag, would resolve one major below the README's own "v0, working but early"
       framing. "preview", dotted, was chosen over "ci"/"dev" (reserved by Arcade for unpublished
       builds) and "beta" (sorts before "preview" under NuGet's own comparer, which would make a
       later move from preview to beta impossible) — see [version-from-git] for the full
       comparer measurement this rests on. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MinVerMinimumMajorMinor>0.1</MinVerMinimumMajorMinor>
    <MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>preview.0</MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>
    <MinVerAutoIncrement>minor</MinVerAutoIncrement>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- [shallow-clone-is-a-defect]: in a shallow clone MinVer sees no tags, computes height 0, and
       silently produces "0.1.0-preview.0" for every commit — no warning, no error. MINVER1001
       does not fire for this case at all, and even where it does fire it is measured to stay a
       warning under TreatWarningsAsErrors above (it is an MSBuild task warning, not a compiler
       one), so neither of those catches it on its own. That is CLAUDE.md's own named
       anti-pattern — "never substitute plausible defaults that let a suite pass while asserting
       nothing" — so CI's fetch-depth: 0 (set on every actions/checkout step in
       .github/workflows/build-and-test.yml) is bought back here with a second, independent guard
       rather than trusted alone: fetch-depth is one line per checkout step that a future edit can
       silently drop.

       A guard keyed on the resolved version string cannot work here: a shallow clone, a
       legitimate untagged root commit, and building entirely outside a git repository (which
       scripts/local-e2e-test.ps1 does by design — see that script's own header for why it packs
       from a non-git copy of src/) were all measured to produce the identical
       "0.1.0-preview.0" string. And a guard keyed on MINVER1001 misses the shallow-clone case —
       the one that matters most — entirely, since that case emits no diagnostic at all. So this
       asks git directly instead of trusting either signal: fail only when a .git is present *and*
       git itself reports the checkout as shallow. A normal (non-shallow) repository and the total
       absence of a .git are both allowed — the latter is exactly what
       scripts/local-e2e-test.ps1's non-git copy needs; MINVER1001 fires there, a warning, by
       construction, and that fallback is accepted for that harness specifically (see this task's
       report for the reasoning).

       Runs BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild" so both `dotnet build` and `dotnet pack` (which depends on
       Build unless run with the no-rebuild option) fail loudly before producing a plausible-looking wrong
       version, rather than a test alone that would only protect people who remember to run
       tests. -->
  <Target Name="InTestEnsureNotShallowClone" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild">
    <Exec Command="git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree"
          IgnoreExitCode="true"
          ConsoleToMSBuild="true"
          StandardOutputImportance="low"
          StandardErrorImportance="low">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_InTestGitRepoCheckExitCode" />
      <Output TaskParameter="ConsoleOutput" ItemName="_InTestGitRepoCheckOutput" />
    </Exec>
    <PropertyGroup>
      <_InTestGitRepoCheckValue>@(_InTestGitRepoCheckOutput)</_InTestGitRepoCheckValue>
      <_InTestIsGitRepo Condition="'$(_InTestGitRepoCheckExitCode)' == '0' and '$(_InTestGitRepoCheckValue)' == 'true'">true</_InTestIsGitRepo>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Exec Condition="'$(_InTestIsGitRepo)' == 'true'"
          Command="git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository"
          IgnoreExitCode="true"
          ConsoleToMSBuild="true"
          StandardOutputImportance="low"
          StandardErrorImportance="low">
      <Output TaskParameter="ExitCode" PropertyName="_InTestGitShallowExitCode" />
      <Output TaskParameter="ConsoleOutput" ItemName="_InTestGitShallowOutput" />
    </Exec>
    <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(_InTestIsGitRepo)' == 'true'">
      <_InTestGitShallowValue>@(_InTestGitShallowOutput)</_InTestGitShallowValue>
    </PropertyGroup>
    <Error Condition="'$(_InTestIsGitRepo)' == 'true' and '$(_InTestGitShallowExitCode)' == '0' and '$(_InTestGitShallowValue)' == 'true'"
           Text="This checkout is a shallow git clone (git rev-parse --is-shallow-repository -> true). MinVer cannot see tag history here and will silently compute a plausible-looking but wrong version (0.1.0-preview.0 for every commit, with no warning) — see [shallow-clone-is-a-defect], docs/superpowers/plans/2026-08-23-trunk-based-versioning.md. Fetch full history before building, e.g. 'git fetch --unshallow', or clone/checkout without --depth. Every actions/checkout step in .github/workflows/build-and-test.yml sets fetch-depth: 0 for exactly this reason — if you hit this in CI, that setting was removed from an existing step or a new checkout step was added without it." />
  </Target>
</Project>